{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790294640,"nonce":"Enw4T3vqtwRM053vhkmWSqxeVrA=","title":"Lua: a suspended or never-started coroutine is never collected","message":"A coroutine that is dropped before it finishes stays alive for the rest of the process. The collector takes every fiber's stack and every address the fiber runtime holds (`held_addresses`, which includes the coroutine record passed as the fiber's env) as roots, so the record, its function and whatever the suspended frames name are always reached. The fiber itself (64 MB reserved stack) is freed only when the coroutine finishes.\n\nEffects:\n- Weak tables: a coroutine created and dropped in a callee never leaves a weak-key or weak-value table, and a `coroutine.wrap` function never leaves a weak-value table. lua5.4 prints `0 0` for the verifier's f3_co.lua probe; zylua prints `11 0` (only the finished coroutine goes).\n- Memory: 20000 coroutines created in a loop, half resumed once to a yield, none kept: lua5.4 2.5 MB peak RSS, zylua (main 678f49eb, NUC) 166 MB.\n\nFix direction: a fiber's stack and env are not roots of their own; they are traced when the coroutine record is marked (as Lua traverses a thread's stack when the thread is marked), and a fiber whose record the sweep frees is released then. That needs the fiber backend to expose per-fiber stack windows keyed by owner, and a free hook for the fiber.","files":null}]}